\begin{figure}

\centering{

\pandocbounded{\includegraphics[keepaspectratio]{images/anatomy.png}}

}

\caption{\label{fig-process}Conceptual summary of the process-based
hierarchy linking food web representations to ecological filters. A
\textbf{global metaweb} represents evolutionarily feasible
consumer--resource links across a defined species pool. Restricting this
pool by spatiotemporal overlap yields \textbf{regional metawebs}, which
differ with regional species composition. Species occurring in both
regions are shown in red, whereas region-specific species are shown in
blue and yellow. \textbf{Realised food webs} emerge when abundance,
consumer diet choice, and non-trophic interaction modification further
filter feasible links under local conditions. The arrows represent
transitions that can be tested separately: compatibility constrains the
global feasible set; overlap constrains the regional set; and ecological
context determines which remaining links occur and how strongly. The
framework predicts nested constraints in principle, while allowing
empirical estimates to differ because of uncertainty, incomplete
sampling, and scale mismatch.}

\end{figure}
